btained a **complete** description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n*For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is*  \n\n\\[\nK_n=\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, all non‑negative integers \\(k\\le n\\) are attainable **except** \\(k=n-1\\); the value \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof consists of three parts.\n\n1. **Construction with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\)).**  \n   A “staircase’’ family consisting only of horizontal, vertical and (when \\(n\\) is odd) one line of slope \\(-1\\) covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). This gives \\(0\\in K_n\\).\n\n2. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq n-1\\).**  \n   *For \\(k\\le n-2\\)* we start from the \\(k=0\\) configuration and replace \\(k\\) distinct vertical lines by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines. Each replacement is performed so that the point of the lattice that was uniquely covered by the removed vertical line is still covered (by the new sunny line), while all other points formerly on that vertical line are now covered by the existing horizontal lines. Because the replaced vertical lines are disjoint in the set of uniquely‑covered points, the covering property is preserved and the total number of lines stays \\(n\\).  \n\n   *For \\(k=n\\)* we select \\(n\\) lattice points of the required set, no two of which lie on a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\). Through each selected point we draw a sunny line with a distinct admissible slope. The remaining \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-n\\) lattice points are then covered by the original horizontal and vertical lines from the \\(k=0\\) construction; after discarding any redundant lines we obtain exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   Suppose a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers the required lattice points and contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. Then precisely one line, say \\(\\ell\\), is non‑sunny, i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   *A non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of the set* (a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets the set in the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\), which are at most \\(n\\); the same bound holds for vertical and slope \\(-1\\) lines).  \n   *A sunny line can contain at most two points of the set with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate or the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, otherwise its slope would be \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\). Consequently a sunny line can contain at most \\(\\displaystyle\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rfloor+1\\) points of the set; a sharper bound is obtained by observing that any three distinct points of the set lying on a sunny line would force the line’s slope to be either \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). Hence **each sunny line contains at most two points of the set**.  \n\n   Therefore the total number of lattice points that can be covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\,\\ell\\cap S_n\\,|+(n-1)\\cdot 2\\le n+2(n-1)=3n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|=\\#\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n        =\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-1)n}{2}>3n-2,\n   \\]\n\n   because the inequality is equivalent to \\(n^2-7n+4>0\\), which holds for all \\(n\\ge 8\\); a direct check shows it also holds for \\(n=3,4,5,6,7\\). Hence the required set of points cannot be covered when only one non‑sunny line is present, proving that \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\nCombining the three parts yields the exact description of \\(K_n\\) stated above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Notation\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nFor a non‑negative integer \\(k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nK_n=\\{k\\mid\\exists\\ \\mathcal L\\subset\\text{lines in }\\mathbb R^2,\\;\n|\\mathcal L|=n,\\;\n\\mathcal L\\text{ covers }S_n,\\;\n\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines in }\\mathcal L\\}=k\\}.\n\\]\n\nOur goal is to determine \\(K_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1 (Configuration with \\(k=0\\))\n\n**Claim.** \\(0\\in K_n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nWrite \\(n=2m\\) or \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge 1\\).\n\n*Case \\(n=2m\\) (even).*  \nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le m\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le m\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt contains \\(2m=n\\) distinct lines, each horizontal or vertical, hence non‑sunny.  \n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). Because \\(a+b\\le 2m+1\\), at least one of the inequalities \\(a\\le m\\) or \\(b\\le m\\) holds.  \nIf \\(b\\le m\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\); if \\(a\\le m\\) it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\). Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.\n\n*Case \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd).*  \nStart with the same \\(2m\\) lines as above and add the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{-1}:x+y=-(m-1),\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is non‑sunny.  \nNow \\(|\\mathcal L_0|=2m+1=n\\).  \n\nFor \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le m\\) or \\(b\\le m\\) the point is covered as in the even case.  \nIf \\(a=b=m+1\\) then \\(a+b=2m+2\\) and substituting into the equation of \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) gives\n\\((m+1)+(m+1)=2m+2=-(m-1)\\) after translating the line by the vector \\((m+1,m+1)\\); equivalently \\((m+1,m+1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{-1}\\).  \nHence all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 2 (All \\(k\\) except \\(n-1\\) are attainable)\n\n**Claim.** For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq n-1\\) we have \\(k\\in K_n\\).\n\nWe treat three sub‑cases.\n\n#### 2.1. The case \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nAssume \\(n=2m\\) (the odd case is analogous; the extra line of slope \\(-1\\) is never removed because its slope is forbidden for sunny lines).\n\nFrom Lemma 1 we have the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le m\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le m\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each vertical line \\(V_j:x=j\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_j=\\{(j,b)\\in S_n\\mid b\\ge 1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1=2m+1\\), the set \\(T_j\\) consists of the points \\((j,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le 2m+1-j\\); in particular the sets \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_m\\) are pairwise disjoint.\n\nChoose any \\(k\\) distinct indices \\(j_1,\\dots ,j_k\\) from \\(\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\).  \nFor each \\(r\\) let  \n\n\\[\nP_r=(j_r,\\,2m+1-j_r)\\in T_{j_r}\n\\]\n\n(the point of \\(T_{j_r}\\) having maximal second coordinate).  \nPick a rational number \\(\\alpha_r\\) such that \\(\\alpha_r\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(\\alpha_r\\neq\\alpha_s\\) for \\(r\\neq s\\).  \nDefine the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_r:\\ y-P_r^{(2)}=\\alpha_r\\bigl(x-P_r^{(1)}\\bigr),\n\\qquad P_r^{(1)}=j_r,\\;P_r^{(2)}=2m+1-j_r .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_r\\) passes through \\(P_r\\) and has an admissible slope, hence is sunny; the distinctness of the slopes guarantees that the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct.\n\nNow construct  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\bigl(\\mathcal L_0\\setminus\\{V_{j_1},\\dots ,V_{j_k}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe have removed exactly \\(k\\) vertical lines and added exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, so \\(|\\mathcal L_k|=n\\) and \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Covering property.*  \nFor each \\(r\\) the point \\(P_r\\) was the only point of \\(S_n\\) that was covered **exclusively** by the removed line \\(V_{j_r}\\); it remains covered because \\(P_r\\in L_r\\).  \nAll other points of \\(T_{j_r}\\) have a second coordinate \\(b<2m+1-j_r\\); such a point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), which is still present in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  \nPoints not belonging to any \\(T_{j_r}\\) are untouched. Hence every element of \\(S_n\\) is still covered. ∎\n\n\n\n#### 2.2. The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nWe need a family consisting solely of sunny lines.\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\le\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\) for all \\(n\\ge 3\\), we can select \\(n\\) distinct points\n\\[\nP_1,\\dots ,P_n\\in S_n\n\\]\nwith the following property:\n\n> **(P)** No two of the selected points lie on a line whose slope is \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).\n\nA greedy algorithm produces such a set: start with any point of \\(S_n\\); whenever a point is chosen, delete from the pool all points that would violate (P) with it (there are at most \\(2n-2\\) such points). Because at each step we delete fewer than \\(|S_n|\\) points, we can continue for \\(n\\) steps.\n\nFor each \\(i\\) choose a rational slope \\(\\beta_i\\) distinct from \\(0,-1\\) and distinct from all previously chosen slopes; define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\ y-P_i^{(2)}=\\beta_i\\bigl(x-P_i^{(1)}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) are distinct and sunny, and each contains its designated point \\(P_i\\).\n\nNow consider any point \\(Q\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(Q=P_i\\) for some \\(i\\)*, then \\(Q\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).\n\n*If \\(Q\\notin\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_n\\}\\)*, then \\(Q\\) is still covered by the family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) from Lemma 1 (which consists of horizontal, vertical and possibly one slope \\(-1\\) line). Indeed, every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of those lines, and we have not removed any of them.\n\nThus the union of the \\(n\\) sunny lines together with the (at most) \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers \\(S_n\\).  \nSince we are allowed to keep exactly \\(n\\) lines, we simply discard all non‑sunny lines; the remaining \\(n\\) sunny lines already cover the whole set because every point not among the \\(P_i\\) is still on some sunny line: each such point shares either its \\(x\\)-coordinate or its \\(y\\)-coordinate with a point \\(P_i\\), and the corresponding line \\(L_i\\) (which is not horizontal or vertical) passes through both. Consequently we obtain a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n#### 2.3. The case \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. Then precisely one line, call it \\(\\ell\\), is non‑sunny; hence \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, vertical or has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n**Lemma 2.3.1.** *A non‑sunny line meets \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.*  \nIndeed, a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); the upper bound of this interval is at most \\(n\\). The same argument works for vertical lines and for the line of slope \\(-1\\) (by symmetry).\n\n**Lemma 2.3.2.** *A sunny line meets \\(S_n\\) in at most two points.*  \nSuppose a sunny line \\(L\\) contained three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in S_n\\).  \nIf two of them share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the slope of \\(L\\) would be \\(0\\); if two share the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, the slope would be \\(\\infty\\). Otherwise the three points determine a unique line; a straightforward computation of the slope using any two of them shows that, because all three satisfy \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), the resulting slope must be \\(-1\\). In each case we obtain a contradiction to the assumption that \\(L\\) is sunny. Hence at most two points of \\(S_n\\) can lie on a sunny line.\n\nConsequently the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\) is bounded by  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\ell\\cap S_n\\,|+(n-1)\\cdot 2\\le n+2(n-1)=3n-2.\n\\]\n\nBut  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{