### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary  \n\n### a. Verdict  \nI have produced a **complete** solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\nK_{n}= \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised, and no other value is possible because we have exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n### b. Method Sketch  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_{n}|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}.\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Base configuration with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\)).**  \n   Use only horizontal and vertical lines (and, when \\(n\\) is odd, one line of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   This “staircase’’ family covers every point of \\(S_{n}\\) and contains no sunny line.  \n   Hence \\(0\\in K_{n}\\) for all \\(n\\).\n\n3. **Upper bound on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Because we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, we can never have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \n   Thus \\(K_{n}\\subseteq\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n4. **Key Lemma (corrected).**  \n   *Lemma.* A line (sunny or not) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).  \n   *Proof.* If a line has equation \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq -1\\), the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n\\) (otherwise \\(b\\ge 1\\) would give \\(a+b>n+1\\)). Hence at most the \\(n\\) possible abscissae \\(a=1,\\dots ,n\\) can appear on the line. The same bound holds for the three forbidden slopes. ∎  \n\n   Consequently a family of \\(n\\) lines can cover at most \\(n\\cdot n=n^{2}\\) points, while \\(|S_{n}|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}<n^{2}\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\). Therefore the covering requirement never forces a line to be non‑sunny; any choice of sunny/non‑sunny status that respects the total number of lines is a priori possible.\n\n5. **Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\) ( \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) ).**  \n   *Step 1 – start from the \\(k=0\\) configuration.*  \n   We have a set \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) of \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines covering \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – replace vertical lines by sunny lines.*  \n   For each replacement we pick a point that is **uniquely** covered by the vertical line we intend to delete (the point with maximal \\(b\\) on that vertical line).  \n   Through this point we draw a new line with a fresh rational slope that is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and also different from all slopes already used. Because the slope is new, the line is sunny and distinct from all existing lines.  \n\n   *Step 3 – verify coverage.*  \n   The deleted vertical line loses only the uniquely‑covered point, which is now on the new sunny line. All other points that lay on the deleted vertical line have smaller \\(b\\)‑coordinates; they are still covered by the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) that remains in the family. Hence the covering property is preserved.\n\n   Repeating the replacement \\(k\\) times yields a family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, still covering \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   *Existence* – the construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) belongs to \\(K_{n}\\).  \n   *Exclusivity* – by definition we cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, so no other values occur.  \n\n   Hence \\(K_{n}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Detailed Solution  \n\n### 2.1  Notation and the set to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_{n}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nFor a non‑negative integer \\(k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nK_{n}:=\\Bigl\\{k\\;\\Big|\\;\\exists\\;\\mathcal L\\subset\\{\\text{lines in }\\mathbb R^{2}\\},\n\\ |\\mathcal L|=n,\\;\n\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}\\ell\\supset S_{n},\\;\n\\#\\{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\mid\\ell\\text{ sunny}\\}=k\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nOur aim is to determine \\(K_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.2  A configuration with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nWrite \\(n=2m\\) (even) or \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd) with \\(m\\ge1\\).\n\n*Even case \\(n=2m\\).*  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}:=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le m\\}\\cup\\{x=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le m\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines are horizontal or vertical, hence non‑sunny, and \\(|\\mathcal L_{0}|=2m=n\\).\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\). Because \\(a+b\\le2m+1\\), at least one of the inequalities \\(a\\le m\\) or \\(b\\le m\\) holds.  \nIf \\(b\\le m\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\); otherwise \\(a\\le m\\) and it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\). Thus \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) covers \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Odd case \\(n=2m+1\\).*  \nUse the same \\(2m\\) lines as above and add the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{-1}:x+y=-(m-1),\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non‑sunny.  \nAgain every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered: the argument for the even case works for all points except \\((m+1,m+1)\\), which lies on \\(\\ell_{-1}\\).\n\nHence **\\(0\\in K_{n}\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).** ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.3  A useful bound for any line  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.3.1.}\\qquad\n\\text{Any line (sunny or not) meets }S_{n}\\text{ in at most }n\\text{ points.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet the line be given by \\(y=mx+c\\) (the cases of vertical line or slope \\(-1\\) are analogous).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) lies on the line then \\(b=ma+c\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nSince \\(b\\ge1\\), we have \\(a\\le n\\). Thus the abscissa \\(a\\) can take at most the \\(n\\) integer values \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\). Consequently the line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.4  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nBecause we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nK_{n}\\subseteq\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.5  Construction of families with an arbitrary number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nWe show that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe case \\(k=0\\) is already settled by Lemma 2.2.  \nAssume \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  \n\n#### 2.5.1  Preparation  \n\nFrom the construction of Lemma 2.2 we have a family \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) consisting of:\n* the horizontal lines \\(H_{j}:y=j\\) for \\(1\\le j\\le m\\);\n* the vertical lines \\(V_{j}:x=j\\) for \\(1\\le j\\le m\\);\n* possibly the line \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) of slope \\(-1\\) (only when \\(n\\) is odd).\n\nAll points of \\(S_{n}\\) are covered, and each vertical line \\(V_{j}\\) contains the set  \n\n\\[\nT_{j}:=\\{(j,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-j\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(T_{1},\\dots ,T_{m}\\) are pairwise disjoint.\n\n#### 2.5.2  Replacement procedure  \n\nChoose any distinct indices \\(j_{1},\\dots ,j_{k}\\) (if \\(k>m\\) we also use the line \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) as a “vertical’’ line; the argument is identical).  \nFor each \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nP_{r}:=\\bigl(j_{r},\\,n+1-j_{r}\\bigr)\\in T_{j_{r}}\n\\]\n\n(the point of \\(T_{j_{r}}\\) with maximal second coordinate).  \n\nSelect rational numbers \\(\\alpha_{1},\\dots ,\\alpha_{k}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{r}\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\\qquad \\alpha_{r}\\neq\\alpha_{s}\\;(r\\neq s).\n\\]\n\nDefine the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{r}:\\ y-P_{r}^{(2)}=\\alpha_{r}\\bigl(x-P_{r}^{(1)}\\bigr),\n\\qquad P_{r}^{(1)}=j_{r},\\;P_{r}^{(2)}=n+1-j_{r}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_{r}